{"id":"g.math.equivalent-expressions.2.ef0fb11153","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"equivalent-expressions","difficulty":2,"prompt":"For $x > 0$, which expression is equivalent to $\\sqrt[5]{x^{3}}$?","choices":["$x^{\\frac{3}{5}}$","$x^{-2}$","$x^{\\frac{5}{3}}$","$x^{15}$"],"answer":"A","explanation":"An $n$th root is the exponent $\\frac{1}{n}$: $\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{\\frac{m}{n}}$. So $\\sqrt[5]{x^{3}} = x^{\\frac{3}{5}}$."},
{"id":"g.math.equivalent-expressions.2.50cddb7f98","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"equivalent-expressions","difficulty":2,"prompt":"For $x > 0$, which expression is equivalent to $\\sqrt{x^{5}}$?","choices":["$x^{3}$","$x^{\\frac{2}{5}}$","$x^{\\frac{5}{2}}$","$x^{10}$"],"answer":"C","explanation":"An $n$th root is the exponent $\\frac{1}{n}$: $\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{\\frac{m}{n}}$. So $\\sqrt{x^{5}} = x^{\\frac{5}{2}}$."},
{"id":"g.math.equivalent-expressions.2.c78862816a","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"equivalent-expressions","difficulty":2,"prompt":"For $x > 0$, which expression is equivalent to $\\sqrt[3]{x^{2}}$?","choices":["$x^{\\frac{2}{3}}$","$x^{6}$","$x^{-1}$","$x^{\\frac{3}{2}}$"],"answer":"A","explanation":"An $n$th root is the exponent $\\frac{1}{n}$: $\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{\\frac{m}{n}}$. So $\\sqrt[3]{x^{2}} = x^{\\frac{2}{3}}$."},
{"id":"g.math.equivalent-expressions.2.be0206af95","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"equivalent-expressions","difficulty":2,"prompt":"For $x > 0$, which expression is equivalent to $\\sqrt[3]{x^{2}}$?","choices":["$x^{\\frac{3}{2}}$","$x^{-1}$","$x^{\\frac{2}{3}}$","$x^{6}$"],"answer":"C","explanation":"An $n$th root is the exponent $\\frac{1}{n}$: $\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{\\frac{m}{n}}$. So $\\sqrt[3]{x^{2}} = x^{\\frac{2}{3}}$."},
{"id":"g.math.equivalent-expressions.2.beb660f730","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"equivalent-expressions","difficulty":2,"prompt":"For $x > 0$, which expression is equivalent to $\\sqrt[3]{x^{4}}$?","choices":["$x^{12}$","$x^{7}$","$x^{\\frac{3}{4}}$","$x^{\\frac{4}{3}}$"],"answer":"D","explanation":"An $n$th root is the exponent $\\frac{1}{n}$: $\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{\\frac{m}{n}}$. So $\\sqrt[3]{x^{4}} = x^{\\frac{4}{3}}$."},
{"id":"g.math.equivalent-expressions.2.3eb6b4c01d","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"equivalent-expressions","difficulty":2,"prompt":"For $x > 0$, which expression is equivalent to $\\sqrt[5]{x^{3}}$?","choices":["$x^{15}$","$x^{\\frac{5}{3}}$","$x^{\\frac{3}{5}}$","$x^{-2}$"],"answer":"C","explanation":"An $n$th root is the exponent $\\frac{1}{n}$: $\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{\\frac{m}{n}}$. So $\\sqrt[5]{x^{3}} = x^{\\frac{3}{5}}$."},
{"id":"g.math.equivalent-expressions.2.a2e7230e68","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"equivalent-expressions","difficulty":2,"prompt":"For $x > 0$, which expression is equivalent to $\\sqrt[3]{x^{4}}$?","choices":["$x^{12}$","$x^{\\frac{3}{4}}$","$x^{7}$","$x^{\\frac{4}{3}}$"],"answer":"D","explanation":"An $n$th root is the exponent $\\frac{1}{n}$: $\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{\\frac{m}{n}}$. So $\\sqrt[3]{x^{4}} = x^{\\frac{4}{3}}$."},
{"id":"g.math.equivalent-expressions.2.40f807f951","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"equivalent-expressions","difficulty":2,"prompt":"The formula $a = \\frac{v - u}{t}$ gives constant acceleration. Which equation correctly expresses $v$ in terms of the other variables?","choices":["$v = \\frac{a}{t} + u$","$v = at - u$","$v = at + u$","$v = a(t + u)$"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Isolate $v$ by undoing the operations on it in reverse order. The result is $v = at + u$. Check by substituting it back into the original formula."},
{"id":"g.math.equivalent-expressions.2.f604fa0b45","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"equivalent-expressions","difficulty":2,"prompt":"The formula $KE = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$ gives kinetic energy. Which equation correctly expresses $m$ in terms of the other variables?","choices":["$m = \\frac{KE}{2v^2}$","$m = \\frac{2KE}{v^2}$","$m = 2KEv^2$","$m = \\frac{v^2}{2KE}$"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Isolate $m$ by undoing the operations on it in reverse order. The result is $m = \\frac{2KE}{v^2}$. Check by substituting it back into the original formula."},
{"id":"g.math.equivalent-expressions.2.e508ff1039","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"equivalent-expressions","difficulty":2,"prompt":"The formula $V = \\frac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h$ gives the volume of a cone. Which equation correctly expresses $h$ in terms of the other variables?","choices":["$h = \\frac{V}{3\\pi r^2}$","$h = 3V\\pi r^2$","$h = \\frac{\\pi r^2}{3V}$","$h = \\frac{3V}{\\pi r^2}$"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Isolate $h$ by undoing the operations on it in reverse order. The result is $h = \\frac{3V}{\\pi r^2}$. Check by substituting it back into the original formula."},
{"id":"g.math.equivalent-expressions.2.20b7fd3afb","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"equivalent-expressions","difficulty":2,"prompt":"The formula $I = Prt$ gives simple interest. Which equation correctly expresses $r$ in terms of the other variables?","choices":["$r = \\frac{Pt}{I}$","$r = \\frac{I}{Pt}$","$r = I - Pt$","$r = IPt$"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Isolate $r$ by undoing the operations on it in reverse order. The result is $r = \\frac{I}{Pt}$. Check by substituting it back into the original formula."},
{"id":"g.math.equivalent-expressions.2.7593af2a62","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"equivalent-expressions","difficulty":2,"prompt":"The formula $a = \\frac{v - u}{t}$ gives constant acceleration. Which equation correctly expresses $v$ in terms of the other variables?","choices":["$v = at + u$","$v = a(t + u)$","$v = \\frac{a}{t} + u$","$v = at - u$"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Isolate $v$ by undoing the operations on it in reverse order. The result is $v = at + u$. Check by substituting it back into the original formula."}
